Prototype ready for review.

Asset: [Mint issuance coordination seam simulator](https://github.com/cashubtc/coco/tree/de620e8/packages/core/operations/mint/prototype-coordination-seam) on `prototype/mint-batching-coordination-seam` (commit [`de620e8`](https://github.com/cashubtc/coco/commit/de620e8)).

Run from that branch with `bun run prototype:mint-coordination-seam`.

The prototype compares processor-owned batching, handler-owned batching, a larger issuance engine, and a minimal internal coordinator. Its recommended seam is a deep, non-public `MintIssuanceCoordinator` beside `MintOperationService`:

```ts
interface MintIssuanceCoordinator {
  schedule(operationId: string, notBefore?: number): void;
  coordinate(): Promise<void>;
  coordinate(operationId: string): Promise<MintOperation>;
}
```

`MintOperationProcessor` retains timer/lifecycle policy, schedules ready operations, and triggers `coordinate()`; only that no-argument processor turn may select compatible peers and create a Mint Batch. Explicit `execute()`/`finalize()` delegate `coordinate(operationId)` immediately, which creates a single-member attempt for an unattached target or joins the exact processor-created attempt already containing it. The coordinator still owns locking, atomic persistence, dispatch, recovery, and concurrent joins. Mint Batch and attempt membership never cross the public interface.

The prototype’s explicit target-first batching scenario is superseded by the processor-only batch-formation rule. Its processor-driven chunk, NUT-29-to-single fallback, competing explicit callers, ambiguous restart recovery, exact attempt resubmission, and NUT-09 proof recovery remain relevant. Formatting and core typecheck pass after building the adapter-test prerequisite.
